Quadrics are classified up to isometries of $RR^n$ (translations and orthogonal transformations about the origin). When $matbold(A)$ is invertible, completing the square with $bold(y) = bold(x) + (1)/(2) matbold(A)^(-1) bold(b)$ gives

$ cal(F)(bold(y)) = bold(y)^tp matbold(A) bold(y) = Q(bold(x)) + k, quad k = (1)/(4) bold(b)^tp matbold(A)^(-1) bold(b) - c, $

so $Q(bold(x)) = 0$ becomes $cal(F)(bold(y)) = k$. Diagonalising $cal(F)$ along the principal axes, the eigenvalues of $matbold(A)$ together with $k$ determine the shape:

- all eigenvalues $> 0$ and $k > 0$: an ellipsoid;
- eigenvalues of both signs and $k != 0$: a hyperboloid;
- one or more zero eigenvalues: linear terms remain and require separate analysis.
